const faq = [
  { q: 'Do ravens recognise individual humans?', a: 'Yes. Research demonstrates that ravens distinguish individual humans. Studies at the Konrad Lorenz Research Station show that ravens treat familiar people (regular handlers, researchers) differently from strangers — with less alarm, greater tolerance, and adjusted social behaviour. Wild ravens in areas with regular human contact (parks, campsites, rural communities) learn to identify specific individuals, remembering whether a person has been a source of food or a threat. This individual recognition likely draws on the same cognitive machinery ravens use for recognising conspecifics.' },
  { q: 'Can ravens form bonds with humans?', a: 'Yes. Ravens raised in contact with humans can form strong individual attachments — following a keeper, responding to their voice, showing distress when separated. Konrad Lorenz described ravens in his care as forming bonds comparable in some ways to social bonds with other ravens. Wild ravens do not typically form close bonds with humans, but habituated individuals in long-established contact areas become familiar and tolerant of specific people. The raven\'s natural social flexibility — bonding with specific individuals within its social group — appears to extend to humans under appropriate conditions.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens afraid of humans?', a: 'Wild ravens show a range of responses to humans, from extreme wariness (neophobia and flight) in hunted or heavily disturbed populations, to remarkable boldness and tolerance in areas where ravens are protected and regularly interact with people. Raven neophobia (fear of novel objects and situations) is a well-documented cognitive trait — but neophobia applies to genuinely novel situations; in habituated individuals, familiar humans do not trigger neophobic responses.' },
];
